# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
#
# THE ACCUSATION-AUTHORSHIP GAP, MADE EXECUTABLE.
#
# The narrative-register decision records that "the record can hold an
# accusation only as an entry with an author" traced to nothing, because the
# harm relations name the alleged offender and the victim and never the writer.
# That is true of their PLACES. This case pins what follows from it, which is
# about their CONSEQUENCES: an accusation nobody signed is not inert, and the
# design's own protections are what it reaches.
#
# Measured 2026-09-16 against the current source. Three of the four routes
# below need no judgment, no grounds, no author and no review. The fourth — the
# voiding rule — does require a review body's judgment beside the lie, which is
# why chapter 1's account of this is stated per route rather than in general.
#
# Nothing here is repaired by this case. It exists so the boundary is
# executable rather than only described, in the same way the collusion case
# pins a wall that is not there.
:expect-pins 19

# ─── 1. The shield, which an unsigned entry takes ────────────────────────────
# Torvi exposed an office holder and holds the shield for it.
? authority(Boss).
# => TRUE
? defend(Torvi).
# => TRUE

# One entry, written by nobody in particular, naming Torvi as having deceived
# the person they exposed. No author, no ground, no date, no review.
deceive(Torvi, Boss).

# The shield is gone — for that exposure. The rule is per pair, which is what
# chapter 4 means by closing against a claim rather than against a claimant.
? defend(Torvi).
# => FALSE

# So a second exposure, of a different office holder, with no accusation
# against it, protects again. The unsigned entry took one claim's protection,
# not the person's.
show(Torvi, Review).
? authority(Review).
# => TRUE
? defend(Torvi).
# => TRUE

# ─── 2. Recognition, which an unsigned entry also takes ──────────────────────
# Marn judged, documented and cited a ground, so the examiner door opens.
? reward(Marn).
# => TRUE

deceive(Marn, Vasha).

? reward(Marn).
# => FALSE

# ─── 3. Severity, which decides where a convicted person is held ─────────────
# Two unsigned accusations about one offender and one victim.
attack(Quilo, Sepp).
cruel(Quilo, Sepp).

? severe(Quilo).
# => TRUE
# It reaches the accused, not the person named as harmed.
? severe(Sepp).
# => FALSE

# And the contrast that makes this the sharpest row in the census. The same
# kind of entry is read by the conviction rule, where it is surrounded by a
# Court judgment, a cited case, a recorded conviction, twelve independently
# witnessed observations, an active custody authorisation and four negative
# guards. None of that is here, so nobody is convicted — while the severity
# that decides where a convicted person is held has already moved.
? prisoner(Quilo).
# => FALSE

# ─── 3a. The same thing, in the shipped cast ────────────────────────────────
# These are not fixture people. Three members of the record this book ships
# carry a severity finding, and every one of them got it from unsigned entries.
? severe(Lalo).
# => TRUE
? severe(Don).
# => TRUE
? severe(Ruk).
# => TRUE

# And the one guard the severity rules do carry, which is not authorship. Hano
# has an attack entry and an injury entry, and they name the same person, so the
# distinctness conjunct is the whole of what stands between Hano and a severity
# finding. Not who wrote them. Not whether anybody reviewed them.
? severe(Hano).
# => FALSE

# ─── 4. Credibility, the one route that does ask for a judgment ──────────────
# Torvi has carried an unsigned deceit entry since the first section. It has
# not voided anybody, because the voiding rule reads a review body's judgment
# beside the lie.
? false(Torvi).
# => FALSE

judge(Review, Torvi).

# Now it voids. The judgment is a real condition and it is the exception, not
# the rule: the three routes above asked for nothing of the kind.
? false(Torvi).
# => TRUE

# ─── What an unsigned accusation still does not do ───────────────────────────
? person(Torvi).
# => TRUE
? owe(State, Eats, Torvi).
# => TRUE
? prisoner(Torvi).
# => FALSE
